PROJECT: COMPANY LABOUR ATTENDANCE & WEEKLY PAYMENT ERP

1. ROLE AND OBJECTIVE

Act as a senior full-stack engineer, database architect, financial calculation specialist, QA engineer, and enterprise UI/UX designer.

Build a production-oriented, full-stack Labour Attendance and Weekly Payment ERP for my company. This system replaces manually prepared labour attendance tables, daily calculations, extra-charge records, weekly payment reports, and monthly summaries.

Do not build only a landing page, static dashboard, clickable mockup, or frontend prototype. Build a functioning application with real authentication, persistent database storage, backend validation, calculation logic, reporting, and role-based authorization.

Accuracy of financial calculations and preservation of historical records are more important than decorative UI.

Work in phases, validate each phase, and fix errors before proceeding. Do not silently skip requirements, fabricate successful tests, or mark unfinished features as complete.

2. TECHNOLOGY STACK

Use the following stack unless an actual platform limitation requires a clearly explained alternative:

- Frontend: React, TypeScript, Vite.
- UI: Tailwind CSS and a consistent reusable component system.
- Icons: Lucide React.
- Backend and database: Supabase with PostgreSQL.
- Authentication: Supabase Auth.
- Authorization: PostgreSQL Row Level Security (RLS), database policies, and server-side validation.
- Data access: Supabase client for authorized queries and database functions or Edge Functions for privileged operations where appropriate.
- Forms and validation: React Hook Form and Zod.
- Data tables: Responsive tables with pagination, sorting, search, and filters.
- Charts: Recharts only where charts improve understanding.
- Date and time: Use explicit date handling and the Asia/Kolkata time zone.
- Testing: Vitest and React Testing Library, plus integration tests for important database and calculation flows.

Use current, mutually compatible package versions.

Keep business logic separate from UI components. Use TypeScript strict mode and reusable services, hooks, validation schemas, and calculation functions.

Never expose Supabase service-role keys, database passwords, or other secrets in browser code. Do not use a service-role key in the client.

If Supabase is not configured yet, explain exactly what needs to be connected. Do not pretend that data persistence, authentication, or RLS works before verifying it.

3. PRODUCT AND USER ROLES

There are exactly two application roles in version one.

A. ADMIN

There must be one designated initial administrator account. The admin has complete access to the company's ERP data and settings.

Admin capabilities:

- View, create, edit, deactivate, and manage staff accounts.
- View all teams, attendance records, rates, expenses, deductions, and advances.
- Create and modify reusable team templates and labour categories.
- Edit any attendance entry.
- Correct calculation inputs and overrides.
- Create, edit, reverse, and merge records subject to audit controls.
- View daily, weekly, monthly, team-wise, and consolidated reports.
- Generate and export PDF and Excel reports.
- Inspect audit logs and previous report versions.
- Configure time rules, overtime, deductions, rounding, and payment settings.
- Reopen previously finalized records when necessary.
- View all historical records.

Do not create a public administrator registration page. Establish the first administrator through a secure, documented bootstrap process or a controlled Supabase administrative setup.

B. STAFF

Staff are authenticated users created or invited by the administrator.

Staff capabilities:

- Create and reuse teams.
- Create daily attendance entries for any number of labour categories.
- Enter counts, start times, end times, and remarks.
- Configure permitted team roles and weekly rates.
- Add extra charges, overtime, advances, credits, and deductions.
- Edit attendance and payment entries.
- Merge attendance records or combine teams in reports.
- View and export permitted daily, weekly, and monthly reports.
- Correct their own entries and other entries within their granted company-wide operational access.

For version one, staff can edit records without requiring routine admin approval. However, preserve a complete audit history. Do not silently overwrite historical financial values.

The admin must always retain full control.

C. LABOURERS

Labourers are NOT application users.

Do not create labourer login accounts, passwords, authentication records, or mandatory personal profiles.

Attendance is count-based. Staff can enter 5 masons, 3 helpers, or any other quantity without recording each worker's name.

Individual labour names must not be mandatory or part of the default workflow.

